---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import RecipeCard from '../../components/RecipeCard.astro'

const install = `npm i -D shotlist playwright`

const others = `pnpm add -D shotlist playwright
yarn add -D shotlist playwright`

const scaffold = `npx shotlist --init`

const first = `npx shotlist example`
---

<Docs
  title="Install"
  description="Installing shotlist, why Playwright is a peer dependency, and scaffolding a first recipe."
>
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold">Install</h1>

  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">Node 20 or newer.</p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={install} lang="bash" />
  </div>

  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">pnpm and yarn work the same way.</p>
  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={others} lang="bash" />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">When you need Playwright</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <span class="font-medium text-ink">Whenever shotlist writes an image.</span>
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">npx shotlist &lt;name&gt;</code>,
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">--all</code> and
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">--check</code> all open a browser — including a
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">source: file</code> recipe, which drives no site but still
    draws its callouts in a page. In practice: install it alongside shotlist and stop thinking about it.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    What runs without it is everything that only reads or writes files:
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">--init</code>,
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">--help</code>,
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">--version</code>, and
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">npx shotlist</code> with no arguments, which lists the recipes
    it found. A CI job that only checks a config parses needs no browser, and no browser is launched for
    it.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    It is an optional peer dependency because its postinstall downloads browsers, and a project that
    depends on shotlist should not pay for that on every install — a CI cache, or a machine that
    only ever reads the recipes, gets nothing for it. When a run needs it and cannot find it, the
    error is the command that fixes it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">A first recipe</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">--init</code> writes a commented
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">shotlist.config.yaml</code> and a first recipe to edit. Neither
    is overwritten, so it is safe to run in a project that already has some.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={scaffold} lang="bash" />
  </div>

  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    Start the site the config points at, then shoot it:
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={first} lang="bash" />
  </div>

  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    The image lands in <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">screenshots/out/</code>, and
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">--install</code> copies it to the destination the recipe
    names.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-10 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    The recipe file carries a
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">yaml-language-server</code> line, so an editor completes
    and validates the keys from the JSON Schemas the package ships.
  </p>
</Docs>
